Z||000000||BBBAD GT||11-04-2001||08:21 AM||||I know there has been some talk about having your AC be your intercooler in a Turbo setup, I've found a pretty good discussion about this topic here:<BR> <A HREF="http://www2.turboford.org/techboard/ubb/Forum1/HTML/006942.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www2.turboford.org/techboard/ubb/Forum1/HTML/006942.html</A> <P>I know somebody asked about this a while back....||||reg||1
Z||000001||Formula88||11-04-2001||01:29 PM||||Well, I'm not saying it can't be done, but in reading that guy's post, he's making a lot of assumptions in his calculations.  Unrealistic assumptions.  Condensor temperature is likely higher than 100*F, evaporator temperature should be no lower than 35*F to avoid icing in the evaporator.  Also, R12 is not a viable medium, since it's not readily available anymore.  If you use R134a, you need to totally redo your calculations.  <P>Basically, he's shown a lot of figures that don't mean anything more than saying it's a neat idea that needs some research.<P>Also, most OEM A/C compressors aren't designed for high rpm usage.  Most have full throttle cutout or rpm cutout to avoid compressor damage, and the drag on the compressor increases with rpm.  I've considered this, but decided it was more difficult to do than the potential payoff.  The way I would consider it is to chill an intercooler before a race so it'll be more efficient during the race.  Sort of like icing the intake manifold.  You cool it down in the staging lanes, then kill the system for your run - getting the benefit of the cooler intercooler.  This doesn't help in everyday street driving.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-04-2001||02:15 PM||||In general, GM AC compressors max out at 6000 RPM and will tolerate spikes of about 7000 RPM. Delphi does make a higher speed version of V5/V7, I think it's goot to 7000(8000peak spike), but I doubt you'll be able to find one. I don't think they make a high speed A series.<P>I'm not aware of most AC systems having an RPM cutout. Most engines simply don't turn that fast stock... When they do it's usually a spike not a cruising RPM.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">   <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||ray b||11-04-2001||04:28 PM||||my idea in a earlyer post was to use the a/c to freeze ice in a intercooler to use up in a quick run at the drags and slowly refeeze over time for next run.<BR>an A/C big enuff to work REALTIME would cost and weight and drag way tooooooo much to work on the street.<BR>i talked to a team meck at a imsa race about real time cooling of turbos and he had tryed it [A/C moved into INTERCOOLER] but even precooled it got toooo hot by first gear shift to work and that was on a small 4 cyl crapie [german ford]<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||11-04-2001||04:53 PM||||Ray is right.There isn't an intercooler efficient enough to reject the heat generated by the compressed air from a turbo.The mass of the intercooler is what the air benefits from in a 1/4 mile race.The best intercooler in efficiency and for space limitations is the liquid to air.According to Spearco the liquid to air intercooler removes heat 9 times faster than air to air.So for 1/4 mile racing a radiator for the intercooler filled with a very cold material is the best way to go.  ||||reg||14
